          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Jane\",\"addressee\":\"St John\",\"anchor_quote\":\"There is no dishonour, no breach of promise, no desertion in the case.\\nI am not under the slightest obligation to go to India, especially with\\nstrangers. With you I would have ventured much, because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you; but I am convinced that, go\\nwhen and with whom I would, I should not live long in that climate.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you—as a sister.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"This is a direct avowal of Jane's loving emotional state toward St John, expressly qualified as familial rather than romantic love. Her references to admiration and trust reinforce the avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.97,\"reason\":\"The focal expression does not itself undertake marriage, missionary service, or any future action. Jane's separate offer to serve as St John's assistant is explicitly distinguished from becoming his wife and must not be transferred onto the love avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I say again, I will be your\\ncurate, if you like, but never your wife.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The expression occurs within a reasoned explanation of why Jane would have trusted St John, not as a reflexive or affective verbal outburst. Its emotional content is adequately captured as an avowal under T.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"With you I would have ventured much, because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.95,\"reason\":\"No residual core force beyond the avowal is needed. The phrase specifies sisterly affection, but it neither performs a separate act nor establishes a new relationship.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"as a sister, I love you\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Jane is resisting St John's claim that she has broken a promise to join missionary work. She explains that her willingness to venture with him arose from admiration, trust, and sisterly love, while continuing to reject marriage and denying any binding obligation to go to India.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression contains no undertaking. In the surrounding exchange, Jane separately offers to accompany St John as an assistant or sister rather than as his wife. St John rejects that arrangement as unsuitable, though he offers to contact a married missionary whose wife needs assistance. Jane also explicitly denies having made a formal promise or incurred an obligation to go to India.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"I recognise the supplied work as Charlotte Brontë's Jane Eyre; the identification is also explicit in the supplied source material. The classification relies only on the supplied evidence.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence strongly establishes the represented force and the sisterly character of the avowal. It cannot independently verify a real person's inner sincerity, but the first-person narration and Jane's stated admiration, confidence, and friendship support actual affection. Later events do not change the force of this utterance.\"}"
